   ],
   "l": "What is at rest is easy to hold;\nwhat has not yet given a sign is easy to plan against.",
   "v": true,
   "n": "易 before a verb reads passively, exactly as 可 does: 易持 is 'easy to be held', never 'easily holds'. The whole opening of the chapter is built on the pattern, four times over, and you will meet it constantly.\n\n兆 is a divination word. The cracks that ran through a heated ox scapula or turtle shell were the 兆, and reading them was how the Shang court asked about tomorrow. The line is saying that a thing announces itself before it arrives — and that the announcement is the moment to act.",
   "p": true
  },
