   ],
   "l": "A tree of a full arm-span's girth\ngrows from a hair's tip;\na terrace of nine storeys\nrises from a heap of earth;\na journey of a thousand li\nbegins under the foot.\nOne who acts on it ruins it;\none who grasps it loses it.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "The tree you cannot get your arms round grows from something no thicker than a hair; the nine-storey terrace rises from piled-up earth; the thousand-li journey begins from the ground under the foot. Whoever works on a thing wrecks it; whoever holds on to a thing loses it.",
   "n": "Three images from three trades — the forester's, the builder's, the traveller's — and all three run the same way, from the finished thing back to the negligible thing it started as. The point is not encouragement. Read on: the very next line says that whoever lays a hand on it ruins it. The tree, the terrace and the road are what happens without anyone forcing them.\n\n足下, 'beneath the feet', is elsewhere a polite way of addressing a superior — one speaks not to him but to the ground he stands on, the way English once said Your Honour. Here it is literal: the ground you are on now.\n\n為者敗之，執者失之 repeats chapter 29 word for word, where it was said of trying to take all under heaven and handle it."
  },
