   ],
   "l": "Therefore the sage: acting without forcing, and so no wrecking;\nno grasping, and so no losing.\nThe people's handling of affairs —\nabidingly, at the coming-near to completion, they wreck it.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "And so the sage acts without forcing, and wrecks nothing; holds on to nothing, and loses nothing. When ordinary people set their hand to something, they wreck it just as it is coming to completion.",
   "n": "從事 is one word — to take a thing in hand — not 從 'follow' plus 事 'affair', and it is one tap-unit here. 民之從事 also shows 之 in a fourth job. It is not 'of' and not 'it': dropped between a subject and its verb, it turns the whole clause into a noun, so that the line reads 'the-people's-taking-things-in-hand' and then comments on it. Once you know the trick you will see it everywhere, and it is the usual way Chinese makes a subordinate clause without a conjunction.\n\nThe observation is exact and unflattering, and it does not spare the reader. The failure comes at 幾成 — not at the start, when nothing has been invested, but at the point where the thing is nearly done and the temptation to seize it is greatest."
  },
