   "l": "Be as careful at the end as at the beginning,\nand then there are no wrecked undertakings.\nTherefore the sage desires the not-desiring,\nand does not prize goods that are hard to come by;",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Take as much care at the finish as at the start, and nothing is wrecked. So the sage's desire is to be without desire; he sets no price on goods that are hard to get;",
   "n": "難得之貨 is a standing phrase of the book's, already met in chapters 3 and 12 — the rare goods a ruler must not chase, because chasing them teaches everyone else to.\n\n欲不欲 is the figure that opened chapter 63 (為無為): the verb takes its own negation as its object. English has to say 'desires to be without desire' and lose the shape."
