   "l": "He learns the not-learning,\nand puts back what the multitude have overshot.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "He learns to be unlearned, and restores what everyone else has gone past.",
   "n": "所過 can be cut two ways. Wang Bi takes 過 as the multitude's excess — those who learn go too far, so the sage learns the not-learning and puts their overshooting right. The other reading takes 所過 as what the crowd passes over and neglects, and 復 as recovering it. The English shifts a good deal between them.\n\nWe follow Wang Bi, as the house rule requires when the primary crib and a contextual reading pull apart, and leave the alternative here on the record. 過 in our text is one character carrying both 'to pass by' and 'to overdo', which is exactly why the line can be read twice."
